This project was written against the public documentation and headers of the JieLi AC79 AIoT
SDK (Apache-2.0, JieLi Technology), and follows the register sequences of its flash, USB and
debug drivers. No SDK source is intended to be included. The SDK files needed to build a
firmware package (uboot.boot, cfg_tool.bin, eq_cfg_hw.bin) are not distributed here.

chip/ota.c speaks the SysEx update protocol of the M-VAVE updater and reads the JieLi update
package header. No vendor code is intended to be included.

Switching the divide-by-zero trap off in chip/fm1_irq.h follows a fix proposed by Steve Pinkham
(spinkham) for Felucca (pull request 111). The code and the comment here are written independently.

chip/usb.c comes from Felucca, where it is GPL-3.0-only; only the include path of one header was changed. Here it
is MPL-2.0: its MIDI clock and transport sync is keremimo's (Felucca commit 019e2ad), licensed under MPL-2.0 by
agreement: https://github.com/hugelton/felucca-bsp/pull/3

The second core (chip/fm1_cpu1.h, chip/fm1_cpu1.S, chip/fm1_cpu1_stop.h, its fault path in chip/fm1_irq.h and
chip/fm1_vec.S) comes from Melodee (https://github.com/keremimo/melodee, GPL-3.0-only there) by Kerem Kilic
(keremimo), licensed under MPL-2.0 by agreement: https://github.com/hugelton/felucca-bsp/issues/5. Its safe hold
adapts Felucca 1.6.1's fm1_cpu1_halt (https://github.com/hugelton/Felucca/pull/247). The units whose second core
misreads shared RAM were measured by Jangada's maintainer (zednaked, https://github.com/zednaked/jangada,
charlesvestal/fm1-x0x#10); those measurements are credited, no code of Jangada's is included.

The history of these files comes from Felucca (https://github.com/hugelton/Felucca), where
they were first published under GPL-3.0-only by the same copyright holder. Each commit
message names the Felucca commit it was taken from. Releases of Felucca that already
contain these files stay under GPL-3.0-only.

